 products and solutions to the drilling, subsea, coiled tubing, well stimulation, and intervention markets, including applications in oil and natural gas, renewable energy, defense, and communications industries. This segment also offers drilling capital equipment and consumable products; subsea remotely operated vehicles and trenchers, submarine rescue vehicles, specialty components and tooling, and technical services; hydraulic fracturing pumps, cooling systems, and high-pressure flexible hoses and flow iron; wireline cable and pressure control equipment; and coiled tubing strings and pressure control equipment, as well as coiled line pipe and related services. The Artificial Lift and Downhole Segment designs, manufactures, and supplies products and solutions for the artificial lift, well construction, production, and infrastructure markets. This segment also offers products designed to safeguard artificial lift equipment and downhole cables; well construction casing and cementing equipment; customized downhole technology solutions, which provides sand and flow control products for heavy oil applications; engineered process systems, production equipment, and separation equipment; and a range of industrial valves. It sells its products through distributors to drilling rig contractors, offshore service companies, governmental organizations, and oil and gas operators and producers. The company was formerly known as Forum Oilfield Technologies, Inc. and changed its name to Forum Energy Technologies, Inc. in August 2010. Forum Energy Technologies, Inc. was incorporated in 2005 and is headquartered in Houston, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Neal A. Lux | President, CEO & Director | Age 50 | Compensation: $1.9M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $466K ranks in the bottom quartile among Energy peers. Each employee generates $38K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Forum Energy Technologies, Inc. is a small-cap energy business with a $882M market cap on $791M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +4% over the past three years and -3% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 2% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 26%.","The business throws off $64M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 8 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 1.6\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 1.8\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 3.9% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 6.9% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.3\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.1) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins softening -1.0pp YoY \u2014 early warning on the moat","sev":"amber","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Operating margins 12% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries a 58% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.5801548609119587},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $3.2M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Value-trap profile (score 60/100): roic below cost of capital; durably derated by the market \u2014 cheapness may reflect deterioration, not mispricing","sev":"amber","cat":"value_trap"},{"text":"ROIC sits in the bottom quartile among Energy peers \u2014 no moat advantage to speak of","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Geopolitical supply shocks \u2014 wars, sanctions, and OPEC decisions create volatility that no hedging programme can fully neutralise.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Energy sector outperforming the broader market over the past quarter","Energy ETF in a sustained uptrend above its long-term average"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"(FET) Volatility Zones as Tactical Triggers","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/17/FET_Volatility_Zones_as_Tactical_Triggers_111126051602_1794392162.html","date":"2026-11-11","timestamp":1794392162.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.273,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Is Forum Energy Technologies (FET) Stock Outpacing Its Oils-Energy Peers This Year?","source":"zacks.com","link":"https://www.zacks.com/stock/news/3021731/is-forum-energy-technologies-fet-stock-outpacing-its-oils-energy-peers-this-year?cid=CS-ZC-FT-fundamental_analysis%7Cyseop_template_1-3021731","date":"2026-11-03","timestamp":1793713203.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.273,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Equities Analysts Issue Forecasts for FET Q1 Earnings","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/03/equities-analysts-issue-forecasts-for-fet-q1-earnings.html","date":"2026-11-03","timestamp":1793707144.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.9,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings","guidance"]},{"title":"Fo